Ask for the replay position when recovery reads a page.

The storage manager passes the pusher's published durable LSN with
every read, and the page service takes zero to mean the latest durable
state. During recovery there is no pusher, so the value is always
zero, and a service that has just anchored answers the latest durable
state with the anchor. The startup process then replays a record onto
a page missing every change since the anchor. On server2 that came
back as "will not overwrite a used ItemId" and a PANIC in redo, three
times in a row, and the node never came up (zou #329).

The startup process knows the LSN it needs, it is replaying it.
lastReplayedEndRecPtr is the end of the last record applied, so a page
carrying everything up to it and nothing of the record in hand is
exactly what redo expects. ZouWalReadLSN returns that during recovery
and the durable LSN otherwise, and the two read paths ask for it.

A read the service cannot cover now parks and then fails loudly, which
is also what should happen when the local pg_wal is ahead of the
stream, the state a SIGKILL'd pusher leaves behind. Today that reads
as a fresh page and corrupts.

diff --git a/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c b/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
--- a/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
+++ b/src/backend/access/transam/zou_wal.c
@@ -28,6 +28,7 @@
 
 #include "access/xlog.h"
 #include "access/xlog_internal.h"
+#include "access/xlogrecovery.h"
 #include "access/zou_wal.h"
 #include "miscadmin.h"
 #include "pgstat.h"
@@ -602,6 +603,35 @@
 	if (!IsUnderPostmaster || ZouWalShmem == NULL || !ZouWalActive())
 		return InvalidXLogRecPtr;
 	return (XLogRecPtr) pg_atomic_read_u64(&ZouWalShmem->durable_lsn);
+}
+
+/*
+ * The LSN a page read has to be served at.
+ *
+ * Normally that is the published durable LSN, see above. During
+ * recovery there is no pusher and so no publication, and the reader
+ * that matters is the startup process, which knows exactly which LSN
+ * it needs because it is replaying it. lastReplayedEndRecPtr is the
+ * end of the last record applied, so a page carrying everything up to
+ * it and nothing of the record in hand is exactly the page redo
+ * expects.
+ *
+ * Zero here means no barrier at all, and a page service that has just
+ * anchored answers it with the anchor. Redo then applies a record to a
+ * page missing every change since, which is a PANIC rather than an
+ * error (zou #329).
+ */
+XLogRecPtr
+ZouWalReadLSN(void)
+{
+	if (RecoveryInProgress())
+	{
+		XLogRecPtr	replayed = GetXLogReplayRecPtr(NULL);
+
+		if (!XLogRecPtrIsInvalid(replayed))
+			return replayed;
+	}
+	return ZouWalDurableLSN();
 }
 
 static void
diff --git a/src/include/access/zou_wal.h b/src/include/access/zou_wal.h
--- a/src/include/access/zou_wal.h
+++ b/src/include/access/zou_wal.h
@@ -37,4 +37,7 @@
 /* The published durable LSN, InvalidXLogRecPtr before the first publish. */
 extern XLogRecPtr ZouWalDurableLSN(void);
 
+/* The LSN a page read must be served at, the replay position in recovery. */
+extern XLogRecPtr ZouWalReadLSN(void);
+
 #endif							/* ZOU_WAL_H */
